# Escapes

@lede
A backslash escapes the next character, treating it as a literal. `\\`
produces a literal backslash.
@end

## The mechanism

```brief
This costs \*5\* dollars.
```

Renders as: This costs *5* dollars. The backslashes are stripped; the
asterisks are literal.

## Where escapes work

Everywhere except inside code spans and code blocks, which are verbatim by
definition. Inside a backtick span the source `\*` is the two literal
characters `\` and `*`.

## What's worth escaping

@dl
Reserved inline sigils
: `*`, `_`, `+`, `~`, `` ` ``, `@`, `\`, `[`, `]`. Escape these when you
  want them to be literal in a position where they would otherwise be
  interpreted.
Reserved line-leading sigils
: `#`, `-`, `>`, `|`, `@`, `/`, `` ` ``, and the digit-then-`.`-then-space
  prefix. Escape the first character if a line starts with one and you do
  *not* want to open a block.
@end

## When you don't need to escape

Most characters do not need escaping most of the time. The word-boundary
rule for emphasis (markers between alphanumerics on both sides are
literal) means `snake_case` and `3*4` already work without escapes.

A backslash that does *not* precede a sigil is a literal backslash —
`a\b` is the three characters `a`, `\`, `b`. The compiler does not error
on "unnecessary" escapes; if the next character could be a sigil, the
escape suppresses interpretation, otherwise it is kept verbatim.

## In code spans

Inside `` `...` ``, no escapes apply. To embed a literal backtick, use
the double-backtick form:

```brief
Run ``echo `whoami` `` to print the user.
```

This is the only verbatim region in the language with a workaround for
"impossible" content. See @ref[language/inline-code.brf](Inline code).

## In LLM mode

Backslash escapes are *resolved* — the rendered output contains the
literal characters the escapes produced, not the escape sequences
themselves.
